Quasistatic limits, diffusion and skin depth (@em-quasistatic: 0, is #chapter, #part-of @electromagnetism, #source "../sources/Electromagnetic-Fields-and-Energy-Haus-Melcher.lingua#r_1VKHEEC14B3VDGBA13T6K4JQNV", #source "../sources/Electromagnetics-Volume-2-Ellingson.lingua#r_5P52CZD00XV6MMPFY9RHADT5KJ", #electromagnetism "../dynamics/Maxwell-equations-and-displacement-current.lingua#r_4ETBPA07D21W3J864XWFXHN4X5", #electrical-power-engineering "../../../engineering/electrical-power-engineering/protection/Fault-current-DC-offset-decrement-and-duty.lingua#r_4SFG3K5ZMNV4JZ95TJXY5543JQ") { r_7RSC1KG4B4DNB7C7319F691MSW
**Read:** [Electromagnetic Fields and Energy — Haus and Melcher](../sources/Electromagnetic-Fields-and-Energy-Haus-Melcher.lingua#r_1VKHEEC14B3VDGBA13T6K4JQNV), quasistatic fields and magnetic diffusion; [Electromagnetics Volume 2 — Steven W. Ellingson](../sources/Electromagnetics-Volume-2-Ellingson.lingua#r_5P52CZD00XV6MMPFY9RHADT5KJ), lossy media and skin depth.

Electroquasistatic and magnetoquasistatic limits retain different dominant terms of Maxwell's equations. Determine their validity from size, time, conduction, displacement and material response, not solely from whether a frequency sounds low. Large structures can have propagation effects at frequencies for which a small laboratory circuit is lumped.

In a homogeneous good conductor with negligible displacement current and linear μ/σ, magnetic diffusion has characteristic scale sqrt(1/(μσω)). For a harmonic plane field the conventional skin depth is δ = sqrt(2/(μσω)). With exp(−iωt), a field penetrating in +z has a decaying factor exp(−z/δ) and a corresponding phase change. State the boundary excitation before comparing amplitudes.

Derive the diffusion equation and skin solution, then compare δ with thickness and curvature radius. Current crowding near edges, proximity effects and magnetic saturation need a fuller solution than a one-dimensional skin formula. Check the good-conductor condition σ ≫ ωε with consistent SI units. At higher frequencies or in dispersive matter, use the full complex constitutive response rather than extrapolating a DC conductivity indefinitely.

**Continue:** [Maxwell equations and displacement current](../dynamics/Maxwell-equations-and-displacement-current.lingua#r_4ETBPA07D21W3J864XWFXHN4X5).

